    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Two cubic equations of state, the Peng-Robinson equation of state (PREOS) and the Schmidt-Wenzel equation of state (SWEOS), were used to calculate equilibrium phase compositions, phase densities, thermal properties, and total volume derivatives for systems containing hydrocarbons, water, and CO2. These calculations are useful for hydrocarbon processing and general purpose reservoir simulation.For hydrocarbon systems, accurate equilibrium phase compositions were determined using methane interaction parameters for both the PREOS and the SWEOS. The methane interaction parameters for the SWEOS were much simpler than for the PREOS.Equilibrium phase compositions for water-hydrocarbon-CO2 systems were calculated accurately for binary, ternary, and quaternary mixtures. For water-hydrocarbon systems, the binary interaction parameters between water (and hydrocarbon components in the aqueous phase were roughly linear functions of temperature (for temperatures greater than 130 O F ), while the interaction parameters in the vapor and hydrocarbon liquid phases were independent of temperature, but depended on hydrocarbon type.The equilibrium compositions of binary mixtures of CO2 and hydrocarbon components were determined accurately using both the SWEOS and the PREOS. The optimum binary interaction parameters between CO2 and hydrocarbon components were determined from experimental composition data for a wide variety of components. For hydrocarbon components lighter than Czo, the interaction parameters for the SWEOS and the PREOS were almost equal.Density calculations using both the PREOS and SWEOlS were improved using the method of volume translation. For the PREOS, volume translation was required for components heavier than CS, while for the SWEOS, translation was only required for components heavier than C16. For the PREOS, a generalized correlation was developed for n-alkanes to determine volume translation factor as a function of temperatureThermal properties such as heat capacity, enthalpy, arid internal energy and volume derivatives were calculated analytically from the EOS. The calculated enthalpies, heat capacities, and sonic velocities were compared with available experimental data. The analytical volume derivatives were virtually identical with the derivatives evaluated numerically. The calculated derivatives were used to evaluate the isothermal compressibilities and thermal expansivities of hydrocarbon mixtures.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
